    "provenance": "Every kgCo2ePerUsd2022 value is the exact \"Supply Chain Emission Factors with Margins\" cell for the listed 6-digit 2017 NAICS code, copied verbatim from the EPA CSV. No value is averaged, interpolated, or estimated.",
    "coverage": "CURATED SUBSET: contains only the 69 NAICS sectors referenced by data/category_to_naics.json (plus the general-retail fallback), NOT the full 1,016-row EPA dataset. Every row is backed by the primary EPA source above.",
    "knownGaps": [
      "ELECTRIC POWER (NAICS 2211xx) is ABSENT from the EPA v1.3 NAICS-6 file (sector 22 contains only natural gas 221210 and water/sewage/steam 2213xx). RENT_AND_UTILITIES_GAS_AND_ELECTRICITY therefore proxies to natural-gas distribution and UNDERSTATES grid electricity. A proper fix needs the commodity-keyed USEEIO dataset. INCOMPLETE for home electricity.",
      "PUBLIC ADMINISTRATION (NAICS 92) is absent (government is I-O final demand, not a commodity); the government-departments Plaid category is treated as non-emission upstream in the crosswalk."
    ],
    "cpi": {
      "index": "CPI-U, All items, US city average, not seasonally adjusted (BLS series CPIAUCNS)",
      "source": "https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/CPIAUCNS",
      "sourceYear": 2022,
      "sourceYearIndex": 292.655,
      "sourceYearIndexNote": "2022 annual average (complete, official).",
      "targetYear": 2025,
      "targetYearIndex": 321.943,
      "targetYearIndexNote": "2025 average over 11 available months (Jan-Dec 2025 excl. Oct; Oct 2025 value was blank in the FRED CPIAUCNS snapshot retrieved 2026-07-17). Difference from a full 12-month average is <0.1%."
    },
    "cpiAdjustmentTo2025": 1.10008,
    "cpiAdjustmentNote": "cpiAdjustmentTo2025 = targetYearIndex/sourceYearIndex = price growth 2022->2025. The engine applies its RECIPROCAL (sourceYearIndex/targetYearIndex ~= 0.90903) to deflate a nominal transaction to 2022 dollars before multiplying by the 2022-dollar factor. Modeling assumption: spend-based emissions track real consumption, so nominal spend is deflated to the factor dollar-year."
  },
